# Documentation Synchronization

## Purpose

Prevent README and roadmap claims from drifting away from the running Spring Boot backend.

## Scope

Apply to `README.md`, `FEATURE_ROADMAP.md`, `structured-project-plan.docs`, `schema.sql` comments/diagrams, examples, and any future architecture or API documentation.

## Mandatory Rules

- Update documentation in the same change whenever implementation affects API paths, headers, bodies, response fields, status codes, configuration, schema, roles, order states, or startup instructions.
- Verify documentation against actual annotations and code. Do not copy stale roadmap statements into new docs.
- Distinguish implemented behavior from planned behavior. Empty authentication/security classes are not an implemented JWT feature.
- Use the actual base prefix `/ecommerce/v1`, actual controller paths, and actual custom health endpoint `/health`.
- Keep environment variable documentation aligned with `application.yaml`: required credentials are `DB_USERNAME`, `DB_PASSWORD`, and `JWT_SECRET`; optional values use the defaults declared in YAML.
- When the database model changes, update `schema.sql`, the roadmap data model, constraints/index descriptions, and setup or migration instructions.
- When an implementation phase changes, update status tables and checklists rather than leaving contradictory “not started” and implemented claims.
- Remove references to deleted frontend/static UI code. Do not recreate frontend instructions unless frontend code is explicitly reintroduced.
- Update Mermaid diagrams when relationships, ownership, or workflow ordering changes; keep node labels and fields consistent with code.

## Best Practices

- Document both legacy and target APIs only when the distinction is explicit and current.
- Include exact runnable Maven and PostgreSQL commands, but use placeholders for credentials.
- Link to source documents using repository-relative Markdown paths.
- Keep examples minimal and executable; do not show insecure default secrets or plaintext production passwords.
- Add a migration note when a change requires consumers or existing database rows to adapt.

## Anti Patterns

- Marking JWT, Swagger, Docker, Redis, Kafka, AWS, or a frontend as present because it appears in a future roadmap.
- Documenting `/` as health when the controller maps `/health`.
- Claiming a `clearCart` endpoint exists without verifying `CartController`.
- Leaving `application.properties` instructions after configuration has been consolidated into YAML.
- Embedding a real secret, token, email credential, or local `.env` value in examples.

## Examples

If `OrderController` changes cancellation from `PATCH /{orderId}/cancel` to another contract, update the README API table, the implemented endpoint section in `FEATURE_ROADMAP.md`, failure/status documentation, and any client migration note in the same patch.

## Checklist

- [ ] Controller routes and docs match exactly.
- [ ] Implemented versus planned status is accurate.
- [ ] Configuration names/defaults match `application.yaml`.
- [ ] Schema, constraints, indexes, and diagrams match entities/database changes.
- [ ] Deleted technologies have no remaining setup instructions.
- [ ] Examples contain placeholders, not secrets.
- [ ] Migration or compatibility impact is documented.
